const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Walgreens Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/walgreens-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Walgreens protein powder third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not that Walgreens publishes. Walgreens Whey Protein Powder Blend carries no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project seal we could find, and SuppCo notes it has not verified product-specific testing. Walgreens also does not advertise a retailer testing program like CVS's. If you need verified testing, choose a product listed in the NSF or Informed Sport databases."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Walgreens have its own brand of protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Walgreens sells Walgreens Whey Protein Powder Blend in vanilla and chocolate 32 oz tubs, listed at $29.99 each on Walgreens.com when we checked. Per the label, two scoops give 30 g of protein and about 180 calories. There is no Walgreens-brand plant protein, isolate or casein; the store also makes its own ready-to-drink nutritional shakes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Walgreens whey protein healthy and clean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is a reasonable protein source but not a clean label. Alongside the whey, the ingredient list includes dextrose, maltodextrin, corn starch, sugar, sucralose, acesulfame potassium and a proprietary creatine, taurine and glutamine blend. Healthy adults can use it, but if you want a short ingredient list, a whey such as Gold Standard or Premier Protein is simpler."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Walgreens protein powder contain creatine?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, a little. Each serving includes a 4.5 g proprietary blend of taurine, creatine monohydrate and L-glutamine, per the label, but the amount of each is not disclosed, so the creatine dose is unknown and probably below the 3 to 5 g a day used in studies. People with kidney disease or on medication should ask a doctor before using it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best flavor of Walgreens protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buyers report the chocolate is the safer pick. Reviewers more often describe the vanilla as artificial or too sweet, while chocolate's cocoa covers the sweetener better. Both flavors rate about 3.5 out of 5 on Walgreens.com. If you plan to blend it into smoothies or oatmeal, the vanilla is more versatile despite those comments. We did not taste either."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the common complaints about Walgreens protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The most common complaints are that it is too sweet, that the vanilla tastes artificial, that the two-scoop serving empties the tub in about 18 shakes, and that it carries more carbohydrate and sugar than a lean whey. Deal-site commenters also point to the maltodextrin and the 4.5 g of non-protein add-ins in every serving."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is protein powder cheaper at Walgreens or Amazon?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Name brands are usually cheaper on Amazon. When we checked, Premier Protein powder cost about 23% more per ounce at Walgreens, the small Orgain tub about 35% more, and Premier shakes up to 74% more per bottle. Optimum Nutrition's small tub was about the same. Walgreens' own whey is not on Amazon and is cheapest on buy-one-get-one weeks."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Store Brands",
    tags: [
      "walgreens protein powder",
      "protein shakes at walgreens",
      "whey protein powder walgreens",
      "walgreens whey protein",
      "is walgreens protein powder good"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Cheap Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-cheap-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Walgreens Protein Powder</Fragment>
      What Walgreens sells under its own name, which name-brand tubs and shakes sit on the same shelf, what each costs per 25 g of protein, and which ones are cheaper on Amazon.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#own-brand">Does Walgreens Have Its Own?</a>
      <a href="#label">Walgreens Whey Label</a>
      <a href="#third-party">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#brands">Brands at Walgreens</a>
      <a href="#vs-amazon">Walgreens vs Amazon Prices</a>
      <a href="#shakes">Protein Shakes at Walgreens</a>
      <a href="#is-it-good">Is It Good?</a>
      <a href="#pay-less">How to Pay Less</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Walgreens protein powder means one store-brand line, Walgreens Whey Protein Powder Blend, a 32 oz tub with 30 g of protein per two-scoop serving that Walgreens listed at $29.99, or about $1.39 per 25 g. Promotions change that math more than anything else. Below: the label, the testing gap, every name brand on the shelf, and the Walgreens versus Amazon price check.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Walgreens Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Walgreens protein powder is a reasonable budget buy if you catch a promotion: Walgreens Whey Protein Powder Blend gives 30 g of protein per two-scoop serving, per the label, and at the $29.99 Walgreens listed for the 32 oz tub it works out to about $1.39 per 25 g of protein. That is cheaper per gram than any name-brand powder on the same shelf at full price, and on a buy-one-get-one-50%-off week it falls to about $1.04.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is the label and the testing. Walgreens' whey adds dextrose, maltodextrin, corn starch and a little sugar, plus an undisclosed blend of creatine, taurine and glutamine, and Walgreens publishes no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport status for it. <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want the cheapest grams of protein in a Walgreens and do not mind a sweeter, carb-heavier scoop. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you are a drug-tested athlete, you want a short ingredient list, or you can wait two days for an Amazon delivery.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product at Walgreens</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Walgreens price seen</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Amazon price seen</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g (Amazon)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Walgreens Whey Protein Blend, 32 oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$29.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not sold on Amazon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.39 (Walgreens)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard, 0.68 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$21.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$19.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.08</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Premier Protein whey powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$42.99 (28 oz)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$29.99 (1.5 lb)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.47</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Orgain Organic Vegan, 1.02 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$26.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$19.98</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.38</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Premier Protein shakes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$15.49 (4-pack)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$26.74 (12-pack)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.86</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Prices are what Walgreens.com and Amazon showed on November 21, 2026, and change by store, flavor and week. Label figures are per the labels at the time of writing and vary by flavor. If price per gram is all that matters, our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> rankings cover the lowest-cost tubs anywhere, store brands included.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Walgreens Whey and the Name Brands Walgreens Stocks That Are Also on Amazon
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first card is Walgreens' own whey. Walgreens-brand products are sold in Walgreens stores, on Walgreens.com and through delivery apps, not on Amazon, so that card has no Amazon link of its own. The other four are products Walgreens shelves that we matched to verified Amazon listings, with the Amazon price we saw on November 21, 2026 and the cost per 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="own-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Does Walgreens Have Its Own Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. Walgreens sells one store-brand protein powder, Walgreens Whey Protein Powder Blend, in vanilla and chocolate 32 oz tubs. Both carry the Walgreens logo, both were listed at $29.99 on Walgreens.com when we checked, and both were available for store pickup at the location the site defaulted to. Older tubs and deal posts call it Walgreens Whey Protein Powder with Creatine and Amino Acids, which is the same product described by what is added to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no Walgreens-brand plant protein, casein, isolate or clear whey. Searching Walgreens.com for protein powder returns around a hundred items, and the only two with the Walgreens label are the vanilla and chocolate whey blends. Everything else on the shelf is a national brand: Optimum Nutrition, Premier Protein, Six Star, Orgain and Garden of Life are the names that appear first. If you want a dairy-free tub at Walgreens, it will be one of the organic plant brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Walgreens does make its own ready-to-drink shakes, and they are a bigger part of the store-brand range than the powder. The cooler and nutrition aisles carry Walgreens High Protein Nutritional Shakes in 11 oz four-packs and Walgreens Plus Protein Nutritional Shakes in 8 oz six- and sixteen-packs, often on buy-one-get-one-free promotions. We cover those in the shakes section below, because they answer a different need from a tub of powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Who makes Walgreens whey is not something Walgreens publishes. Store-brand supplements are usually made by contract manufacturers, and the Walgreens formula, with 30 g of protein in a 50 g serving plus creatine, taurine and glutamine, looks very like the 30 g fitness whey blends sold under other grocery and pharmacy store labels. That is a resemblance, not a confirmed supplier, so we do not name one. It matters less than it sounds: what decides whether a store-brand whey is a good buy is its label and price, which we can check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Walgreens Whey Protein Powder Blend?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Walgreens whey gives 30 g of protein, about 180 calories, 9 g of carbohydrate and 2.5 g of fat in a two-scoop, 50 g serving, per the label as reproduced by SuppCo and FatSecret at the time of writing. The tub holds about 18 servings. Walgreens.com did not load its product pages for us during research, so treat these as label figures from third-party databases and check the panel on the tub you pick up; formulas and flavors can differ.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein comes from a whey blend, which on budget tubs usually means mostly whey protein concentrate with some isolate. Thirty grams in a 50 g scoop is 60% protein by weight. For comparison, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard is about 77% protein by weight (24 g in 31 g) and Premier Protein's powder is close to that. The rest of the Walgreens scoop is the carbohydrate, fat, flavoring and the add-in blend.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is the creatine, taurine and glutamine blend?</h3>

          <p>
            Each serving includes a 4.5 g proprietary blend of taurine, creatine monohydrate and L-glutamine, per the label listing. Proprietary means the label gives the total but not how much of each is inside. That makes it impossible to know whether you get a useful dose of creatine; the dose studied for strength training is about 3 to 5 g a day, and a 4.5 g blend shared with two other ingredients almost certainly holds less. If you want creatine, buy plain creatine monohydrate separately and dose it properly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sweeteners, carbs and allergens</h3>

          <p>
            The inactive ingredients listed include dextrose, maltodextrin, corn starch, sugar, cocoa in the chocolate, natural and artificial flavors, sucralose and acesulfame potassium, plus thickeners such as cellulose gum and gum arabic and soy lecithin. Buyers in a Slickdeals thread about the tub flagged the same points: the added sugar, the maltodextrin and the 4.5 g of non-protein add-ins. None of that is unsafe, but it is why the scoop runs 180 calories where a cleaner whey gives about 120 to 150 for a similar dose of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            It contains milk, and the soy lecithin means it is not a fit for strict soy avoidance. Anyone with kidney disease, who is pregnant, or who takes medication should ask a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a creatine-containing powder or a large daily protein dose. The same goes for older adults with medical conditions who are using powder or shakes to hit a protein target.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Walgreens Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not that Walgreens publishes. Walgreens Whey Protein Powder Blend carries no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport, Informed Choice or Clean Label Project seal that we could find, and SuppCo, which scores supplements partly on verified testing, notes it has not verified product-specific testing for it. That is the main thing separating it from the better name brands on the same shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            This puts Walgreens behind CVS on paper. CVS runs a retailer program, Tested to Be Trusted, that requires third-party testing of the supplements it sells, which we covered in our <a href="/cvs-protein-powder/">CVS protein powder</a> guide. Walgreens does not advertise an equivalent program for its store-brand protein. All supplements sold in the US must follow FDA manufacturing rules, but those rules rely on the manufacturer's own testing, not an independent lab checking each batch.
          </p>

          <p>
            If testing matters to you, the name brands at Walgreens give you more. Optimum Nutrition describes Gold Standard as banned-substance tested, and NSF's Certified for Sport registry lists some Optimum Nutrition products; check the registry for the exact flavor and size before relying on it. Garden of Life, also on Walgreens' shelf, sells some sport-certified products, though the organic protein Walgreens lists is certified organic rather than sport certified. Our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition review</a> covers what its testing does and does not include.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people, the practical risk with an untested budget whey is not a banned substance; it is that nobody outside the company has confirmed the protein count or checked for heavy metals. If you compete in a tested sport, use only a product listed in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases. If you do not, Walgreens whey is a reasonable risk at its price, but it is not the safest choice in the aisle.
          </p>
        </div>

